From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] dwrite: a glyph drawn at a fraction of a pixel lands there; Chromium's letters evenly spaced

VS Code for Windows showed its interface text with uneven letter spacing at
every display scale -- "Fun dam entals", "Custom ize your editor" -- and so
did Chrome, Edge and every other Electron or Chromium program.

Chromium draws text with Skia, and Skia draws each glyph on its own: it
places glyphs at quarter-pixel positions, asks DirectWrite's glyph run
analysis for the glyph's image at the position's fraction (0, 1/4, 1/2 or
3/4 pixel, given as the dx of the run's transform), and puts that image at
the whole pixel. IDWriteGlyphRunAnalysis turned each glyph's origin into a
whole pixel by truncating it (OffsetRect takes integers), so the fraction
was dropped: every glyph was drawn up to 3/4 pixel left of its place, each
by another amount, and the gaps between letters came out a pixel wider or
narrower at random.

The analysis now splits each glyph's origin into the whole pixel its image
is placed at and the fraction (in 1/16 pixel steps) its outline is moved by
before FreeType measures and renders it, for both the texture bounds and
the texture; the glyph cache keeps one bitmap per fraction. Aliased glyphs
are placed at the nearest whole pixel. Embedded bitmap glyphs cannot move
by part of a pixel and are left at the whole pixel.

Test: test/dwspacing-gate.sh with test/dwspacing-probe.c, 64- and 32-bit,
Liberation Sans and Inter at 12-21 px (mutant: SG_MUTANT_DWSPACING in
dlls/dwrite/font.c and freetype.c; it fails every check).
---
--- a/dlls/dwrite/dwrite_private.h
+++ b/dlls/dwrite/dwrite_private.h
@@ -579,6 +579,8 @@
     RECT bbox;
     BYTE *buf;
